<Base
  title="The Search for Arthur"
  eyebrow="Australian Red Cross Wounded and Missing Enquiry Bureau · AWM 1DRL/0428 · read 9 September 2026"
  dek="Twenty-three pages of men trying to find out what happened to one private soldier at Pozières — including a friend who was still looking a year later, from a lecture room at New College, Oxford.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The official record disposes of Arthur Hartley Sneyd in one line: <em>killed in
    action, 20 August 1916, in the field, France</em>. Underneath that line is a file nobody in this
    family appears to have read, and it does not agree with itself. Six men were asked what happened
    to him. They gave six different answers, and the last one — arriving thirteen months late — is the
    only one that explains why he has no grave.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>What the Bureau was.</strong> The Australian Red Cross Wounded and Missing Enquiry
      Bureau existed because the army's own machinery could not answer the question families actually
      asked: <em>what happened to him?</em> Its searchers went through the hospitals and base depots of
      France and England asking men who had been there. The statements are hearsay, contradictory and
      often wrong. They are also the only surviving voices of the men who were beside him.
    </Note>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">He was <strong>5050 Private Arthur Hartley Sneyd, 9th Battalion</strong>
    — a typist from Wooloowin, Ivy Sneyd's younger brother, and so Cristina's great-great-uncle. He had
    joined the battalion in the line on <strong>29 July 1916</strong>, giving up his sergeant's rank to
    do it. <a href={u("/great-war/")}>His service →</a></p>
  </section>

  <Blk label="Étaples · 3 November 1916" title="First: killed by a shell">
    <blockquote>
      His initials are A.H., and his number — 5050. He belonged to B.Co. I knew him personally. As I
      was coming out of the trenches, wounded, at Pozières, Pte. R. Kent, B.Co. told me he had seen
      Sneyd killed by a shell. Kent is still with the batt., I think.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Pte. P. R. Rhead, 4280, C.Co.,
      No. 1 A.D.B.D., Étaples, 3 November 1916. A note in the margin: <em>Written Pte Kent 25.11.16</em>
      — the Bureau wrote to Kent, as it always did.</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Étaples · 4 December 1916" title="Then: lifted out and put over the parapet">
    <blockquote>
      I saw Sneyd and two others struck, and as I considered, killed, by the same shell. We were in a
      communication trench on the Somme, and the bodies of the three were lifted out and placed over
      the parapet. <strong>I afterwards heard a rumour that Sneyd proved not to be dead, and was seen
      to get up.</strong> He was certainly never reported at D/S or hospital, and I have no doubt
      whatever that he was dead when lifted over the parapet.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— L/Cpl. Clark, 1328, A.Coy.,
      No. 1 Canadian General Hospital, Étaples, 4 December 1916.</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">He reports the rumour and then, in the same breath, refuses it. That sentence is
    the file in miniature.</p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Étaples · 4 December 1916" title="&quot;His death is mysterious&quot;">
    <blockquote>
      Sneyd was of my Co. — A. <strong>He held the position of Sgt. in Australia before he came
      out.</strong> His death is mysterious. He was <em>found for dead</em> on the parapet but
      apparently he was only suffering from shell shock. He was taken down the line and I heard that he
      died in hospital. The best man to refer to about him is <strong>Cpl. Walsh, his great chum</strong>.
      Walsh is now in training for an officer at the Cadet School, Cambridge, and he knows all
      particulars of Sneyd. Sneyd's pay-book, disc, etc. are at our Bn. H.Q. out here.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Pte. Geo. Holloway, 5377,
      A.Co., 1 Convalescent Depot, Étaples, 4 December 1916. <em>Written Cpl Walsh 19.12.16.</em></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">A stranger to this family, in a convalescent depot in France, independently
    confirms the thing the Statement of Service records in a clerk's shorthand: <strong>he had been a
    sergeant and gave it up.</strong> And he names the man who will spend the next year looking.</p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="No. 9 General Hospital, Rouen · 16 December 1916" title="And then the account that changes everything">
    <blockquote>
      I knew Sneyd, his No. was 5050. On the 20th August, I was one of the working party with Sneyd in
      the trenches <strong>just in front of Pozières</strong>. A shell burst over us and killed two or
      three men. Sneyd was one of those to fall. It was at first thought that he was dead, and he was
      picked up and rolled over on the parados.
      <strong>The next morning he had recovered, and I spoke to him. He had a bandage round his head
      and he was carrying a white flag behind some stretcher bearers who were getting in the wounded
      from No Man's Land.</strong> I have neither seen nor heard of him since. If he has been killed it
      must have been on the way back to the dressing station as we were being very heavily shelled by
      the Germans at the time.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Cpl. A. Lee, 1370, A.Co.,
      2 Pl., No. 9 General Hospital, Rouen, 16 December 1916.</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p>A man counted among the dead, rolled onto the back wall of the trench and left. And in the
    morning he was up, with his head bandaged, walking out into No Man's Land <strong>behind stretcher
    bearers, carrying a white flag</strong>, to help bring in other people's wounded.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">A second man tells the same story from the other side of it:</p>
    <blockquote>
      In August he was blown up by a shell in the trench; the pioneers thought him dead and put him over
      the parapet. <strong>Next morning he crawled in to the trench.</strong> Cpl. Kendrick told me this
      after we came out of Pozières in August.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— reported at Le Havre,
      4 January 1917, from Cpl. H. Kendrick, 1st A.D.B.D. <em>Written Cpl Kendrick 11.1.17.</em></footer>
    </blockquote>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="No. 4 Officer Cadet Battalion, New College, Oxford · 6 January 1917" title="Walsh">
    <p>Holloway had named him: <em>Cpl. Walsh, his great chum</em>. The Bureau wrote to him. By the
    time the letter found him he was out of the line and in training for a commission at Oxford, and
    this is what he sent back.</p>
    <blockquote>
      I am sorry to say that I can give no absolutely definite word of his death. On the afternoon of
      <strong>Sunday Aug. 20th about 6 p.m.</strong> was the last time I saw him, as he was going up to
      the front line on fatigue duty. About 1 a.m. Monday when I was going up the communication I was
      told he was killed. Next morning I heard that he was seen walking along with stretcher-bearers
      with his head bandaged. <strong>On coming out of the trenches I at once made exhaustive inquiries
      from stretcher bearers, medical officers etc, but all to no avail.</strong> The only answer I got
      was that he was reported killed and that his private belongings were handed in to the 9th
      battalion Orderly room. It appears that he was killed in the communication trench by the
      concussion of a shell and he was left on the side of the trench for dead.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>A. H. Sneyd was a lad of 23 years, about 5ft 6ins in height, thin features and fair
      hair.</strong>
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Pte. Geo. Walsh, A.Coy.,
      No. 4 Officer Cadet Battalion, New College, Oxford, 6 January 1917.</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">The description is a friend's memory, not a medical officer's tape: the attestation
    paper measured him at <strong>5 ft 7½ in with dark brown hair</strong>, and he was
    <strong>22</strong>, not 23. Walsh had not seen him for five months and was writing from a
    lecture room in Oxford. It is the closest thing to a portrait of him that exists.</p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="France · September 1917" title="Thirteen months later, Walsh finds the answer">
    <p>He did not let it go. By September 1917 he was a <strong>lieutenant</strong>, and he had tracked
    down a sergeant of a different battalion who had been there.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Re Pte. H.A. Sneyd, 5050. <strong>He was killed at Mouquet Farm on the 22.8.16 and was buried in
      a shell hole in No Man's Land just out of the old German Trench, near where 10th Battalion
      Headquarters is. A rough cross (wooden) was placed on his grave.</strong>
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— letter from Sgt. Morton,
      10th Battalion, France, 1 September 1917.</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      Please find enclosed report by Sgt. Morton of 10th Battn re death of 5050 A.H. Sneyd.
      <strong>It seems to clear up all the mystery about it. If I ever get another chance I shall
      certainly endeavour to locate the grave.</strong>
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— letter from Lieut. Geo. Walsh,
      France, 19 September 1917.</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <Note>
      <strong>He never got another chance, or the chance came to nothing.</strong> There is no grave.
      Arthur Hartley Sneyd is commemorated on the <strong>Australian National Memorial at
      Villers-Bretonneux</strong>, which carries the names of Australians who fell in France and whose
      graves are not known. A rough wooden cross in a shell hole in No Man's Land, in ground fought
      over for two more years, was not a marker that survived the war.
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the file settles, and what it unsettles</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li><strong>He was not killed instantly.</strong> Two independent witnesses — Lee, who spoke to
      him, and Kendrick, who was told at the time — have him surviving the shell that was thought to
      have killed him and being up the following morning with his head bandaged.</li>
      <li><strong>The last thing anyone saw him doing was helping.</strong> Walking behind stretcher
      bearers, carrying a white flag, going out for the wounded, under heavy shelling.</li>
      <li><strong>He had been a sergeant and gave it up</strong> — confirmed here by a man in a
      convalescent depot who had no reason to invent it, and again by the family's own Roll of Honour
      particulars, which say he <em>"gave up this rank in Egypt in order to get across to France"</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>The date may be wrong.</strong> Every official record says <strong>20 August
      1916</strong>. Sgt. Morton, the only witness who says he saw the burial, says
      <strong>22 August</strong> — and names <strong>Mouquet Farm</strong>, not Pozières. Both are
      consistent with Lee's account of a man who survived the 20th. The archive keeps 20 August,
      because that is what the army certified and what is carved at Villers-Bretonneux, and records
      Morton's 22nd beside it. <span class="chip dis">unresolved</span></li>
      <li><strong>His company is unresolved.</strong> Rhead says B Company; Holloway and Lee say A.</li>
      <li><strong>He was buried, once.</strong> In a shell hole in No Man's Land, just outside the old
      German trench, under a wooden cross. Somebody took the trouble.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <Blk label="Brisbane, 20 August 1919 — three years to the day" title="What the family called him">

    <p>On the third anniversary the family put notices in both Brisbane papers. They are short, and
    the first two words of each are the whole point.</p>

    <blockquote>
      SNEYD.—In loving remembrance of <strong>Q.M.S. Arthur Sneyd</strong>, killed in action at
      Pozières, August 20th, 1916. Inserted by his loving father, sisters, and brother.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— <em>The Brisbane Courier</em>,
      Wednesday 20 August 1919, p. 6 · <span class="mono">nla.news-article20379031</span>. The
      <em>Telegraph</em> carried the same notice the same day.</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <Note>
      <strong>Q.M.S.</strong> — Quartermaster Sergeant. He had reverted to the ranks on 29 July 1916
      to get into the line, and he died a <strong>private</strong>. That is what the army recorded and
      what is carved at Villers-Bretonneux.
      <br /><br />
      Three years later his father, his sisters and his brother were still calling him by the rank he
      gave away. <span class="chip inf">the exact appointment is unconfirmed</span> — his Statement of
      Service shows corporal and then sergeant in the depot battalion, and whether "Quartermaster
      Sergeant" was his formal appointment or the family's version of it is not settled here.
    </Note>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem">Directly beneath it in the <em>Courier</em>, a second notice, signed
    with one name and no relationship:</p>

    <blockquote>
      SNEYD.—In loving memory of Q.M.S. Arthur Sneyd, killed in action at Pozières, August 20, 1916.
      <strong>Death divides, but memory clings. Ruby.</strong>
    </blockquote>

    <p class="small">There is no Ruby in this family tree. A memorial notice signed with a first name
    alone, placed separately from the family's, three years after the death, is the form a
    <strong>sweetheart</strong> takes — but the archive cannot show that, and says so.
    <span class="chip lore">unidentified</span> She is the only person connected to Arthur Hartley
    Sneyd whose surname nobody now knows.</p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The other brother</h2>
    <p class="dek">Reading this file turned up something the archive had entirely missed. Arthur was
    not the only Sneyd brother in France. <strong>Vivian Claude Sneyd</strong> (1895–1949), the next
    boy down, served with the <strong>3rd Field Ambulance</strong> and came home with the
    <strong>Military Medal and Bar</strong> — decorated twice for bravery, as a stretcher-bearer.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">This archive had him down as a <em>salesman</em>, on the
    strength of a death notice. Nothing on this site said he had been in the war at all.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>His citation has now been read, and it is the same job.</strong> At
      <strong>Westhoek, east of Ypres, on 4 October 1917</strong>, in charge of a squad of bearers,
      "<em>this man volunteered to go back during the barrage fire of an evening counter attack, and
      returned with rations and stretchers</em>" — carrying up what the forward aid posts needed
      through the dark and the mud, at "<em>very considerable danger of being lost</em>".
      <br /><br />
      Fourteen months earlier, his brother had last been seen walking into No Man's Land behind
      stretcher bearers with a white flag and a bandage round his head.
      <a href={u("/great-war/")}>Vivian's service, and the citation in full →</a>
    </Note>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a href={u("/great-war/")}>The two D'Arcy men of 1916 →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/sneyd/")}>The Sneyds →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/sources/")}>Where this comes from →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
